Honour the metadata checksum seed (INCOMPAT_CSUM_SEED)

A volume made by e2fsprogs 1.47 or later sets EXT4_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_CSUM_SEED,
because mke2fs now enables metadata_csum_seed by default. On such a volume every
metadata checksum is seeded from the superblock's s_checksum_seed rather than
from the filesystem UUID -- and every checksum here was seeded from the UUID
directly, in ext4_balloc.c, ext4_dir.c, ext4_dir_idx.c, ext4_extent.c,
ext4_fs.c and ext4_ialloc.c.

So the volume is refused at mount, and correctly so: ext4_fs_check_features()
finds an incompatible bit outside CONFIG_SUPPORTED_FINCOM and answers ENOTSUP.
The effect is that no ext4 volume a current Linux creates can be read at all,
which is how this was found -- a 7 GiB mkfs.ext4 partition, straight from KDE
Partition Manager, reported "incompat 000022c2, unsupported 00002000" and
nothing else.

The fix is the one the kernel makes: keep the seed in one place, s_csum_seed,
which is s_checksum_seed when the feature is set and crc32c(~0, uuid, 16)
otherwise. The eight sites that computed the second inline now ask for it, and
the bit joins the supported set -- seven in files any build compiles, and
ext4_xattr.c's so that the change is whole rather than conditional on a build
option.

Two notes for a maintainer:

- ext4_types.h gains a named checksum_seed at its on-disk offset, 0x270, taken
  out of the padding in front of checksum so the struct stays 1024 bytes. The
  same file's EXT4_FINCOM_BG_USE_META_CSUM is renamed EXT4_FINCOM_CSUM_SEED:
  0x2000 in the incompatible field is CSUM_SEED, and "use crc32c for bg" is not
  what it means.

- ext4_bg_crc16() is deliberately left alone. That is the legacy GDT_CSUM path,
  and the kernel seeds it from the UUID as well.

Tested read-only, which is the direction it was needed in, on a stock mkfs.ext4
volume: mounting it, listing it, reading a file, and asking for free space.
Those reads are themselves the proof the seed is right, since
ext4_balloc_verify_bitmap_csum(), ext4_dir_csum_verify() and the extent tail
check all verify against what the filesystem stored rather than against anything
this patch computes twice.

--- a/include/ext4_super.h
+++ b/include/ext4_super.h
@@ -50,6 +50,27 @@ extern "C" {
 #include <ext4_types.h>
 #include <ext4_misc.h>
 
+/**@brief   The seed this filesystem's metadata checksums start from.
+ *
+ * ESPIX addition. ext4 seeds a metadata checksum with either a value derived
+ * from the filesystem UUID or -- when the incompatible CSUM_SEED feature is set
+ * -- a value stored in the superblock, and e2fsprogs 1.47 and later set that
+ * feature by default. So without this a volume made by any current Linux cannot
+ * be read: the store-side and verify-side functions in ext4_balloc.c,
+ * ext4_dir.c, ext4_dir_idx.c, ext4_extent.c, ext4_fs.c and ext4_ialloc.c all
+ * seeded from the UUID inline, and every verification of a bitmap, directory
+ * block or extent tree then failed against a checksum computed from the other
+ * seed.
+ *
+ * Upstream refuses the feature instead of honouring it. That refusal is
+ * honest -- it is what "incompatible" means -- but it also means upstream reads
+ * only old volumes, so this file carries the difference deliberately. Remove it
+ * if the change goes upstream; see docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md.
+ *
+ * @param   s superblock descriptor
+ * @return  the seed, as crc32c already folded it */
+uint32_t ext4_sb_csum_seed(struct ext4_sblock *s);
+
 /**@brief   Blocks count get stored in superblock.
  * @param   s superblock descriptor
  * @return  count of blocks*/
